Primary key vs unique constraint 


PRIMARY KEY	UNIQUE
Nulls allowed?	❌ No (must always have a value)	✅ Yes (one NULL allowed)
Per table limit	Only 1 primary key	Many unique columns allowed
Auto-increment?	Commonly used with AUTO_INCREMENT	Rarely auto-increment
Index?	✅ Always creates an index	✅ Always creates an index
Purpose	Identifies the row (the "ID")	Prevents duplicate values 

id          INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,   -- one per table, no NULLs
    email       VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE,   -- no duplicate emails, but can be NULL

Why Only One PRIMARY KEY Per Table
The primary key's job is to uniquely identify a row. If you had two primary keys, the database wouldn't know which one is "the" identifier for the row.

The Real Reason (Technical)
Reason	Explanation
Unambiguous identity	A row must have exactly ONE way to be identified. Two PKs = two ways = confusion.
Internal indexing	The DB uses the PK as the row locator (like a phone book's index). It can only use one column as that index.
Foreign keys reference it	Other tables point to YOUR PK. If you had two, which one do they reference?
SQL standard	The SQL spec defines PK as a single constraint on a column set, not multiple independent constraints.

What You CAN Do Instead
If you need two columns together to be unique:

-- Composite primary key (still counts as ONE primary key)
PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id)

This is one primary key made of two columns — the combination is unique, not each column individually.
